WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.460
Marcus has a life policy and worries about keeping it funded

2
00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:05.620
if he becomes unable to work.

3
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:10.580
A disability-related life rider can address that concern, but its title

4
00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:11.720
alone is not enough.

5
00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.620
Identify the person whose disability matters, the definition of qualifying

6
00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:21.960
disability, the required duration, the age limits and

7
00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:27.320
the benefit provided. Losing a job is not automatically a qualifying

8
00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:32.220
disability. Being approved under another program does not automatically

9
00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.640
satisfy this rider's definition either.

10
00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:39.980
These are supplemental benefits attached to life insurance.

11
00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:45.740
We are studying their life-policy functions here, not building a separate

12
00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:50.540
health-insurance course. Read the issued rider before promising that a

13
00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:55.827
benefit applies. A waiver-of-premium rider waives the premiums specified by

14
00:00:55.827 --> 00:00:59.067
the rider when the insured meets its qualifying disability conditions.

15
00:00:59.507 --> 00:01:03.167
Suppose Marcus's hypothetical rider approves waiver of a one hundred dollar

16
00:01:03.167 --> 00:01:07.327
monthly base premium. That covered premium no longer has to come

17
00:01:07.327 --> 00:01:09.587
from his household during the approved benefit period.

18
00:01:09.947 --> 00:01:12.127
It does not mean the insurer sends him an extra one

19
00:01:12.127 --> 00:01:13.587
hundred dollar spending check.

20
00:01:14.007 --> 00:01:18.367
Review which premiums and riders are included, when waiver begins and

21
00:01:18.367 --> 00:01:22.247
when it ends. The contract may require premiums during the waiting

22
00:01:22.247 --> 00:01:25.887
and claim-review period, with later reimbursement or credit if its terms

23
00:01:25.887 --> 00:01:29.347
provide that result. Do not tell a client to stop paying

24
00:01:29.347 --> 00:01:30.847
merely because a claim was submitted.

25
00:01:32.197 --> 00:01:37.037
Universal life separates premium payments from the charges deducted inside

26
00:01:37.037 --> 00:01:42.717
the policy. A cost-of-insurance or monthly-deduction waiver may

27
00:01:42.717 --> 00:01:45.617
waive the stated charges during qualifying disability.

28
00:01:46.697 --> 00:01:48.797
Read precisely which charges are covered.

29
00:01:49.677 --> 00:01:53.857
Suppose Marcus normally plans to pay two hundred dollars monthly, while

30
00:01:53.857 --> 00:01:56.781
the covered monthly deduction is sixty dollars.

31
00:01:56.781 --> 00:02:01.357
Waiving sixty dollars of deductions is not the same as depositing

32
00:02:01.357 --> 00:02:03.697
the full two hundred dollar planned payment.

33
00:02:04.437 --> 00:02:08.517
It does not automatically fund every future growth assumption or eliminate

34
00:02:08.517 --> 00:02:13.217
every lapse risk. Policy loans, investment results,

35
00:02:14.136 --> 00:02:18.097
unwaived charges and other terms can still matter.

36
00:02:18.937 --> 00:02:22.477
An actual monthly-deduction rider may cover more than the cost of

37
00:02:22.477 --> 00:02:27.271
insurance alone. A disability income benefit attached to life insurance

38
00:02:27.271 --> 00:02:31.411
provides the specified income payment when its conditions are met.

39
00:02:31.971 --> 00:02:34.951
It may be combined with a waiver, but those are still

40
00:02:34.951 --> 00:02:39.661
separate benefits. In a hypothetical example, a rider pays two hundred

41
00:02:39.661 --> 00:02:44.131
fifty dollars of monthly income and also waives a sixty dollar

42
00:02:44.131 --> 00:02:49.150
policy charge. The spendable income is two hundred fifty dollars.

43
00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:53.111
The sixty dollar waived charge is not another cash payment to

44
00:02:53.111 --> 00:02:58.491
the client. Read the income amount, benefit duration, waiting period

45
00:02:59.071 --> 00:03:03.571
and termination conditions. Do not assume the rider replaces the insured's

46
00:03:03.571 --> 00:03:07.191
entire salary or continues for life simply because the base life

47
00:03:07.191 --> 00:03:11.660
policy is permanent. Elena owns a life policy on her child

48
00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:13.200
and pays its premiums.

49
00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.120
A juvenile payor benefit can protect those premium payments if the

50
00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.620
covered adult payer dies or becomes disabled as specified in the

51
00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:26.860
rider. The child is the insured under the base life policy,

52
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.660
while the payor rider addresses the adult's qualifying event.

53
00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:36.300
Suppose this hypothetical rider continues covered premiums only until the

54
00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:41.400
child's twenty-first birthday. That age belongs to the stated rider, not

55
00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:45.540
a universal rule. A parent's death does not trigger the child's

56
00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.820
base death benefit while the child is living.

57
00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:53.220
Confirm which adult is covered, the rider's eligibility requirements and the

58
00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:58.405
actual ending conditions. A sample rider requires total disability

59
00:03:58.405 --> 00:04:02.225
for six consecutive months before benefits qualify.

60
00:04:03.325 --> 00:04:06.945
An insured disabled for two months has not yet met that

61
00:04:06.945 --> 00:04:12.085
particular duration requirement. Another rider may use different terms.

62
00:04:12.975 --> 00:04:17.145
Distinguish the waiting period from a maximum benefit period and distinguish

63
00:04:17.145 --> 00:04:21.885
the insured's age limit from a child's payor-benefit ending age.

64
00:04:22.525 --> 00:04:27.145
The producer should help the client identify the proper claim process

65
00:04:27.145 --> 00:04:30.425
and required proof without inventing approval.

66
00:04:31.265 --> 00:04:36.205
For an exam scenario, trace the same sequence each time: identify

67
00:04:36.205 --> 00:04:40.385
the covered person, confirm the stated trigger, apply the timing conditions

68
00:04:41.045 --> 00:04:42.665
and identify the exact benefit.

69
00:04:42.665 --> 00:04:47.925
Waived premiums, waived charges, income and payor protection

70
00:04:47.925 --> 00:04:50.025
are related but different.
